    "reason": "The assertion that similarity search 'uses' a distance metric is generally true for vector-space models, but not universally sufficient. Counterexample: Hash-based locality-sensitive hashing (LSH) or exact nearest neighbor structures using bit-vectors may rely on Hamming distance or direct bitwise operations without computing a continuous 'distance metric' in the traditional Euclidean/Cosine sense",
    "counterexample": "A system using MinHash LSH for similarity search relies on Jaccard coefficient estimation via hash collisions rather than calculating a standard distance metric like cosine similarity between dense embeddings.",
